WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:41.000
This is episode 6 of Not Built For This, Maximum Temperature. Last summer, temperatures in Phoenix, Arizona got above 110 degrees for more than 30 days in a row. A couple of times in July, the thermometer nearly cracked 120. It is always hot in Phoenix in the summertime, but this was a new and unwelcome record. And as the city endured this string of unbearably hot days, there was this one image that I became fixated on. All right, check this out. Even the saguaros here in the valley can't take the heat anymore.
这里是《不适于此》第六集：《最高气温》。去年夏天，亚利桑那州凤凰城的温度连续30多天超过110华氏度（约43.3摄氏度）。在7月的几次极端天气中，温度计几乎爆表，直逼120华氏度（约48.9摄氏度）。凤凰城的夏天向来炎热，但这创下了令人不安的新纪录。正当这座城市忍受着这一连串热得令人窒息的日子时，有一个画面深深吸引了我的注意。好吧，瞧瞧这个。连山谷里的巨人柱仙人掌也再也经受不住这酷热了。

2
00:00:41.000 --> 00:01:17.000
It's become a summer trend that just won't seem to stop. Our beloved saguaros toppling to the ground and it's leaving many neighbors— All across the city, saguaros were keeling over in the heat. These massive, stately cacti can grow up to 60 feet tall and weigh thousands of pounds. And they were coming down left and right, crushing cars and denting roofs. I just started hearing a loud crack. So I looked up and I just saw the cactus falling over. A good amount of the roof, like the drywall, fell off. Our next-door neighbors thought it was like a minor earthquake.
这已经成了一种挥之不去的夏季趋势。我们深爱的巨人柱仙人掌纷纷倒地，这让许多居民——在全城各地，巨人柱在酷热中成批地倒下。这些巨大、庄严的仙人掌可以长到60英尺（约18米）高，重达数千磅。它们接二连三地倒塌，压坏了汽车，砸凹了屋顶。我只听到一声巨响。所以我抬头一看，就看见仙人掌倒了下来。很大一部分屋顶，比如干墙，都掉了下来。我们的邻居还以为发生了小型地震。

3
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:45.000
The saguaro is a master of desert survival. There's literally nothing about its physical being, from its waxy skin to its spongy interiors, that isn't adapted to Arizona's furnace blast temperatures. And yet here they were, dying. Well, I thought it would outlive me by a hundred years. It seemed like a pretty ominous sign. If the saguaros couldn't survive in the heat, what did it mean for us?
巨人柱是沙漠生存的大师。从它打蜡的表皮到海绵状的内部，它的生理结构无一不适应亚利桑那州如炼狱般的极端高温。然而它们却在这里死去。嗯，我曾以为它能比我多活一百年。这似乎是一个非常不祥的征兆。如果连巨人柱都在酷热中无法生存，那对我们意味着什么？

4
00:01:45.000 --> 00:02:17.000
I first got interested in heat in Phoenix. I had been covering climate change for 20 years and didn't give heat much thought, as strange as that may seem. This is the climate writer Jeff Goodell. He's the author of the book The Heat Will Kill You First. And he got interested in heat when he was in downtown Phoenix in the middle of summer and decided to walk 20 blocks to a meeting. Rookie move. I mean it was like walking into a kind of solid wall of this invisible force
我最初对凤凰城的酷热产生兴趣时，我已经报道气候变化20年了，却对热浪不怎么关注，听起来可能有点奇怪。这是气候作家杰夫·古德尔（Jeff Goodell）。他是《热浪杀机》（The Heat Will Kill You First）一书的作者。他对热浪产生兴趣，是因为有一次他在盛夏时节来到凤凰城市中心，决定步行20个街区去开会。初来乍到的蠢行。我的意思是，那感觉就像是走进了一堵由无形力量构成的坚固墙壁，

5
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:55.000
that just surrounds you and envelops you and kind of suffocates you and makes your body, I mean the only word I can use to kind of describe it is freak out. And by the time I got to my meeting I was dizzy, my heart was pounding and I was a little off balance. And I realized, oh my gosh, heat is dangerous. If I had to go another 20 blocks, I don't know if I would make it. So for me, that was the beginning of everything. Specifically, the beginning of Jeff trying to understand
它包围着你、包裹着你、几乎让你窒息，让你的身体——我的意思是，我能用来形容它的唯一词汇就是“崩溃”。当我到达开会地点时，我已经头晕目眩，心跳加速，甚至有点站不稳了。我意识到，天哪，热浪太危险了。如果我再走20个街区，我不知道自己还能不能坚持下来。所以对我来说，那就是一切的开始。具体来说，是杰夫开始试图理解

6
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:28.000
what climate change will mean for us on a physiological level. The impacts of climate disasters are often assessed in terms of property damage. When a hurricane hits a place like Florida, the news shows images of gas stations ripped open like tin cans, homes flattened by the wind, cars floating down a flooded main street. We talk about the millions of dollars of damage. But in places like Phoenix and in hot places all over the world,
气候变化将在生理层面上对我们意味着什么。气候灾难的影响通常是用财产损失来评估的。当飓风袭击佛罗里达等地时，新闻画面中会出现被撕裂得像易拉罐一样的加油站、被风夷为平地的房屋、在被淹没的主干道上漂浮的汽车。我们讨论的是数百万美元的损失。但在凤凰城以及世界各地的炎热地区，

7
00:03:28.000 --> 00:04:00.000
climate change is wreaking a very different kind of havoc. On living things. On people's bodies. Heat kills more people every year than any other weather event. The best estimate on the annual death toll is around 500,000 people globally, but that's almost certainly an undercount. And yet, even as we're freaking out about our warming world, many of us still don't really think about heat itself as an existential threat. And I think that's at
气候变化正在造成一种截然不同的破坏。对生物的破坏。对人类身体的破坏。每年死于酷热的人数超过任何其他天气事件。据对全球年度死亡人数的最佳估计，大约为50万人，但这几乎可以肯定是保守估计。然而，尽管我们对不断变暖的世界感到惊慌失措，许多人仍然没有真正将热浪本身视为一种生存威胁。我认为这

8
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:34.000
least in part because of the language that we used for many years to describe this crisis. The phrase global warming doesn't not sound very alarming. It sounds like maybe we should get our beach chairs and go to the beach. It sounds like time for shorts and flip-flops. In other words, if our global temperatures increase by, say, a few degrees, is that really something to get all bent out of shape about? No, it doesn't sound dangerous at all. It just sounds like, you know, I have other bigger problems to worry about than whether
至少部分是因为我们多年来用来描述这场危机的语言。全球变暖（global warming）这个词听起来并不那么令人担忧。它听起来可能像是我们该拿上沙滩椅去海滩了。听起来像是穿短裤和人字拖的时候了。换句话说，如果我们全球的气温上升，比如说几度，这真的是一件值得大惊小怪的事吗？不，听起来一点也不危险。听起来就像是，你知道的，我还有其他更大的问题要操心，而不是

9
00:04:34.000 --> 00:05:26.000
it's 78 or 80 degrees outside. But a few degrees do matter. Last year, 2023, was the hottest year ever recorded in human history. The last time the world was this hot was 125,000 years ago. And as we build on a hotter baseline, heat waves also get more extreme and more deadly. Phoenix, Arizona has the dubious distinction of being the hottest major city in America. And it's perhaps the best place in the country to investigate our confused relationship with heat and its effects on our health. It's a place that's marketed itself as a warm and pleasant escape from the snowbound north.
外面是78度还是80度。但几度确实至关重要。去年，即2023年，是人类有记录以来最热的一年。上一次世界这么热还是在12.5万年前。随着我们在一个更热的基准线上不断刷新纪录，热浪也变得更加极端、更加致命。亚利桑那州的凤凰城有一个令人尴尬的“荣誉”——它是美国最热的大城市。它也许是全国审视我们对热及其对健康影响的混淆关系的绝佳地点。这个地方把自己营销为一个摆脱北方冰雪的温暖宜人避难所。

10
00:05:26.000 --> 00:06:00.000
But now there are people in the Valley of the Sun looking at these warming trends, and they're worried. As Phoenix continues to grow, with thousands of new residents arriving every year, there are advocates pushing the city and even the federal government to recognize extreme heat for what it is. A force that is testing our biological limits. Because our bodies were not built for this. This is the final episode in our series.
但现在，太阳谷（Valley of the Sun）的人们正看着这些变暖的趋势，他们感到担忧。随着凤凰城的不断发展，每年都有成千上万的新居民涌入，一些倡导者正在推动市政府甚至联邦政府正视极端热浪的真面目——一种正在考验我们生理极限的力量。因为我们的身体并非为此而生。这是我们系列的最后一集。

11
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:40.000
I'm Emmett Fitzgerald. 99PI senior editor Delaney Hall is also our in-house desert correspondent. She lives in New Mexico. And last year, she made a trip to Phoenix to try and understand how heat went from being the city's number one selling point to a public health crisis. Last summer, on a 100 degree day, I went out driving around Phoenix with a guy named Sergio Armandades. So, are we going to some Circle Ks? Yeah, we have a list here of store addresses.
我是埃米特·菲茨杰拉德（Emmett Fitzgerald）。99PI的高级编辑德兰妮·霍尔（Delaney Hall）也是我们的内部沙漠通讯员。她住在新墨西哥州。去年，她前往凤凰城，试图了解热浪是如何从该市最大的卖点变成一场公共卫生危机的。去年夏天，在一个100华氏度（约37.8摄氏度）的日子里，我跟着一个名叫塞尔吉奥·阿尔曼达斯（Sergio Armandades）的男子开车在凤凰城四处转悠。所以，我们要去一些Circle K便利店吗？是的，我们这里有一份店铺地址清单。

12
00:06:40.000 --> 00:07:11.000
I'll check in with the manager and I'll let him know that I'm here doing outreach. Sergio does street outreach with the Phoenix Rescue Mission. It's an organization that works with homeless people in Phoenix. And today I'm tagging along with him while he hits up a number of convenience stores. And is there a reason people kind of tend to group around the circle case? Like what's going on there from a homeless person's perspective? You know, on a hot day, you can grab a bunch of ice and grab some soda.
我会去跟经理打个招呼，让他们知道我来这里做外展服务。塞尔吉奥在“凤凰城救援使命”（Phoenix Rescue Mission）从事街头外展工作。这是一个为凤凰城无家可归者提供服务的组织。今天我跟着他一起走访了多家便利店。人们倾向于聚集在Circle K周围有什么原因吗？从无家可归者的角度来看，那里的情况是怎样的？你知道的，在炎热的日子里，你可以拿一堆冰块，再弄点苏打水。

13
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:36.000
You know, it's pretty cheap. Polar pops is my favorite thing. So if you got food stamps, you can go get some quick food real quick, you know? It's like turning into a Circle K advertisement. I love it. I love it. food real quick, you know. It's like turning into a Circle K advertisement. The city of Phoenix is trying all sorts of ways to make itself cooler as the planet heats up. They're painting streets with reflective coating to repel the sun,
你知道，这挺便宜的。北极泡泡冰（Polar pops）是我的最爱。所以如果你有食品券，你就可以马上去买点快餐，你知道的？这快变成Circle K的广告了。我太喜欢了。我太喜欢了。快餐，你知道的。这快变成Circle K的广告了。随着地球变暖，凤凰城市政府正在尝试各种方法让自己降温。他们在街道上涂上反光涂层来反射阳光，

14
00:07:36.000 --> 00:08:14.000
they're planting lots of trees, and they're building out a network of cooling centers where people can go hang out during the heat of the day. They've even got a new heat czar whose job is to help cool the city down. But when it comes to saving lives during the hottest summer in history, at least the hottest one so far, it's hard to imagine anyone with a more important job than Sergio. We're gonna go get some ice. I think it's important that I have some cold water. It's a good icebreaker. No pun intended, right?
他们种植了大量树木，并正在建立一个降温中心网络，供人们在白天的酷热时段前往休憩。他们甚至有了一位新的“热浪沙皇”（heat czar），其职责就是帮助这座城市降温。但当谈到在有史以来最热的夏天——至少是迄今为止最热的夏天——拯救生命时，很难想象还有谁的工作比塞尔吉奥更重要。我们要去弄点冰块。我觉得随身带点冷水很重要。这是一个很好的破冰方式。双关语，不是吗？

15
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:40.000
Sergio is a remarkably chill guy. He drives all over the city, cranking the AC, cracking jokes, and talking about Jesus. But his work also has high stakes. Every summer, hundreds of people in Phoenix die from the heat, and many of them are homeless. Sergio knows firsthand how hard it can be to survive when you have nowhere to go to escape the heat.
塞尔吉奥是一个非常随和的人。他开车穿梭于全城，开着强劲的空调，开着玩笑，谈论着耶稣。但他的工作也面临着巨大的风险。每年夏天，凤凰城都有数百人死于酷热，其中许多人是无家可归者。塞尔吉奥深知，当无处可去以躲避酷热时，生存是多么艰难。

16
00:08:40.000 --> 00:09:08.000
I've slept at this park before. He did it himself for five years. My stomping grounds weren't too far from here. And now he works in a lot of the same neighborhoods where he once camped or hung out or spent hours looking for scraps of shade. When we pull up at the Circle K, there are a couple people hanging out at the side of the store. Sergio approaches a man to see about getting him into a shelter for the night, while I
我以前在这个公园睡过。他自己曾露宿街头五年。我以前的活动范围离这儿不远。现在他在许多曾经露宿、闲逛或花几个小时寻找阴凉之地的街区工作。当我们把车停在Circle K旁时，有几个人在店侧面闲逛。塞尔吉奥走近一个男子，看能否安排他当晚入住庇护所，而我

17
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:30.000
sit and talk with a middle-aged woman. She has a repurposed baby stroller packed full of her stuff, including a cute scruffy little dog. What's that little guy's name? That's Cupacabra. Hey, he goes nuts when he sees another dog. This is Bunny. She came to Phoenix in 1986 and then says that she got stuck.
坐下来与一位中年妇女交谈。她有一个改装过的婴儿车，里面塞满了她的东西，还有一只可爱、毛茸茸的小狗。那只小家伙叫什么名字？那是丘帕卡布拉（Chupacabra）。嘿，他一看到别的狗就发疯。这是邦尼（Bunny）。她于1986年来到凤凰城，然后说自己被困在这里了。

18
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:53.000
She's been living here for almost 40 years, homeless on and off, and so by now she's developed a lot of tricks for beating the heat. She carries an umbrella, she wears a rag dipped in cold water around her neck, and she makes sure she stays hydrated. You know, all we can do is try to find a shady spot to sit if the cops don't run us out. Most times, we're stuck out in the sun. It really sucks,
她在这里断断续续地流浪了近40年，所以到现在她已经摸索出了许多对抗酷热的绝招。她随身携带雨伞，脖子上围着一块浸过冷水的抹布，并确保自己补充足够的水分。你知道的，我们能做的就是尽量找个阴凉的地方坐坐，如果警察没有把我们赶走的话。大多数时候，我们只能呆在太阳底下。这真的很糟糕，

19
00:09:53.000 --> 00:10:19.000
because there's nowhere out there to really cool down. It's hot, nasty, muggy, dirty, filthy, just all around bad news. You know, I feel like Arizona has this thing where people come because they love the sun and they love the life here. Have you found that or? I've found a few people that like it, yeah. But there's very few of them that like it whenever it hits 120, 125.
因为外面根本没有地方可以真正凉快下来。天气又热、又恶心、又闷热、又脏、又臭，总之到处都是坏消息。你知道，我觉得亚利桑那州有一种现象，人们因为热爱阳光、热爱这里的生机而来到这里。你发现是这样吗，还是？我确实发现有一些人喜欢它。但当气温达到120度、125度（约48.9至51.7摄氏度）时，喜欢它的人就寥寥无几了。

20
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:46.000
Then they want to scurry inside where their air conditioning is, you know. We're out here 24-7. They get to go home in their nice, comfortable air conditioners. Bunny has hit on a central fact about life in modern Phoenix, which is that the heat affects people in profoundly unequal ways. For some, it's an asset. For some, it's an asset.
那时候他们就想急忙逃回有空调的室内，你知道的。而我们24小时都在外面。他们却可以回到舒适的空调房里。邦尼触及了现代凤凰城生活的一个核心事实，那就是酷热对人们的影响存在着极大的不平等。对某些人来说，这是一笔资产。对某些人来说，这是一笔资产。

21
00:10:46.000 --> 00:11:07.000
For others, it's a liability. And this inequity goes back a long way in Phoenix's history. Nearly a thousand years ago, indigenous people found ways to live with the heat of the Sonoran Desert. They created cliff dwellings where the temperatures were cooler. They built homes with thick walls of stone and mud.
对其他人来说，这是一个负担。这种不平等在凤凰城的历史由来已久。将近一千年前，原住民就找到了适应索诺拉沙漠酷热的方法。他们建造了气温较低的悬崖住所。他们用厚厚的石头和泥土建造房屋。

22
00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:39.000
And for centuries, the population of central Arizona stayed relatively small. The extreme heat provided a check on development, and Phoenix remained a sleepy outpost. But fast forward to the 1950s and 60s and that began to change. City boosters began selling Phoenix precisely because of its climate. They pitched it as a land without winter where you could play golf year round. And it turned out that hundreds of thousands of people
几个世纪以来，亚利桑那州中部的人口一直保持在相对较小的规模。极端的高温对城市发展起到了抑制作用，凤凰城一直是一个安静的前哨站。但快进到20世纪50和60年代，情况开始发生变化。城市推销员开始极力推销凤凰城，正是因为这里的天气。他们把它宣传为一个没有冬天的土地，在那里你可以全年打高尔夫球。事实证明，成千上万的人

23
00:11:39.000 --> 00:12:08.000
wanted in on that vision. And if it got a little toasty in the summertime, there was a new-fangled technology that could help you with that. So you can find people in the early 60s talking about living, you know, completely air-conditioned lives. This is Andrew Needham, a historian who studies the environmental and indigenous history of the American West. And Andrew told me about this old article he found in the Saturday Evening Post.
都渴望实现这一愿景。如果夏天变得有点热，有一种新奇的技术可以帮你解决这个问题。所以你可以在60年代初的人们谈论过着，你知道的，完全空调化的生活。这是安德鲁·尼德姆（Andrew Needham），一位研究美洲西部环境与原住民历史的历史学家。安德鲁跟我讲了他在《星期六晚邮报》上发现的一篇旧文章。

24
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:30.000
It included an interview with a local banker. He boasted about how he got through the summer days. He says, well, I wake up in my air-conditioned room, I get in my air-conditioned car, I go to the air-conditioned garage of my building, I go to my air-conditioned office. At night, I go to an air-conditioned garage of my building. I go to my air-conditioned office. At night, I go to an air-conditioned restaurant. Maybe I see a movie in an air-conditioned theater.
其中包含对当地一位银行家的采访。他吹嘘自己是如何度过夏天的。他说，嗯，我在有空调的房间醒来，我坐进有空调的车里，我去我大楼有空调的车库，我去我有空调的办公室。到了晚上，我去我大楼有空调的车库。我去我有空调的办公室。到了晚上，我去有空调的餐厅。也许我会在有空调的影院看电影。

25
00:12:30.000 --> 00:13:01.000
And so, you know, this kind of life entirely encased in the summer in refrigerated air conditioning is a reality for people like bankers. But while bankers were bathing in this mechanically cooled air, working-class people in Phoenix were living much hotter lives. Back in the 1960s, the city was starkly divided by race and class and by temperature. When a reporter from the New Republic went to Phoenix to write about the city,
所以，你知道，这种在夏天完全被冷气包裹的生活，对银行家这样的人来说是现实。但当银行家们沐浴在机械降温的空气中时，凤凰城的工人阶级过着热得多的生活。早在20世纪60年代，这座城市就因种族、阶层和温度而产生了鲜明的划痕。当《新共和国》杂志的一名记者前往凤凰城撰写关于该市的文章时，

26
