00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:31.000
And they would have brought in more pathologists, but there weren't any. It doesn't seem like an easy job to just staff up, you know what I mean? Your skills are so specialized, there's probably not just a bunch of like temp medical examiners hanging around Phoenix. Definitely, yeah. If we have a vacancy, it's on average 20 months for us to be able to fill it. All offices across the country are really struggling with that and have been for the better part of two decades.
所以你可以把病理学家加入我们已经不堪重负、在气候变化重压下摇摇欲坠的基础设施名单中。我们的住房不达标，我们的保险系统没有准备好。我们的道路、桥梁和堤坝正在老化且不堪一击。而我们的法医非常、非常疲惫。凤凰城的热浪日益显现为一场可预测的年度灾难。但令人惊异的是，联邦政府并不这么看。极端高温并不在FEMA（联邦紧急事务管理局）有权应对的灾难名单之列。人们对热浪的风险和危险存在着巨大的低估，

53
00:28:31.000 --> 00:29:18.000
So you can add pathologists to the list of our already overburdened infrastructure buckling under the weight of climate change. Our housing is inadequate, our insurance systems aren't ready. Our roads and bridges and levees are aging and flimsy. And our medical examiners are very, very tired. Heat in Phoenix increasingly appears to be a predictable annual disaster. But amazingly, that's not the way the federal government sees it. Extreme heat is not on the list of disasters that FEMA is authorized to respond to. There's been a vast underestimation of the risks of heat and the dangers of heat that
以至于连FEMA的人都还落后于形势，没有重新评估我们所生活的这个新气候世界的风险。这部分是因为许多热浪的受害者仍然不为人见。全国乃至全球各地与热浪相关死亡的数据依然不够充分。而且热浪杀死了许多生活在社会边缘的人。他们并不是那些大声疾呼要求获得更多保护的人。但这也是因为我们的政府看待灾难的基本方式。它是面向对物品的破坏，而不是对人的伤害。

54
00:29:18.000 --> 00:30:02.000
even the people at FEMA are still behind the curve in recalculating the risks of this new climate world that we're living in. And that's partly because so many of HEAT's victims are still unseen. The data on HEAT-related deaths across the country and around the world remains inadequate. And HEAT kills a lot of people who are living on the margins. They're not the people that are out there lobbying for more protection. But it's also because of the fundamental way that our government thinks about disasters. It's geared towards damage to stuff rather than damage to people.
在灾难发生后权衡如何发放资金时，政府绝大多数将财产价值作为衡量标准。从某些方面来看，这种计算是有道理的。调查与热相关的死亡事件既困难又耗时。当你谈论超负荷的社工、苦于应付庞大患者群体的医疗系统，或者当一个人在滚烫的人行道上白白死去时所失去的东西时，很难将损失量化。但这意味着凤凰城以及全国各地的炎热地区，每年夏天在热浪夺走可怕的人命时，基本上只能靠自己。

55
00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:30.000
The government overwhelmingly uses property value as a metric when weighing how to dish out funds after disasters happen. And in some ways, that calculation makes sense. It's hard and time-consuming to investigate heat-related deaths. And it's hard to quantify damage when you're talking about overstretched social workers, or a hospital system struggling to keep up with its patient load, or what's lost when a person needlessly dies
